    {
      "title": "Is it time to change the UK age of consent? – podcast",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2026/nov/20/is-it-time-to-change-the-uk-age-of-consent-podcast",
      "description": "<p>After Russell Brand’s conviction and accusations against David Walliams, campaigners are calling for a staggered age of consent. <strong>Nosheen Iqbal</strong> speaks to the columnist <strong>Polly Hudson</strong> and the educator <strong>Michael Conroy</strong> about what needs to change</p><p><strong>Send your comments and questions to </strong><a href=\"mailto:tif.questions@theguardian.com\"><strong>tif.questions@theguardian.com</strong></a></p><p>Anger, disgust, shame. In the last couple of weeks, as news of famous older men being convicted and accused of crimes against very young women and girls has emerged, the public mood has shifted.</p><p>The conversation has moved from “how did society let this happen?” to “how do we stop it from happening again?”. In that space, a campaign has grown for the consent gap to be closed.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2026/nov/20/is-it-time-to-change-the-uk-age-of-consent-podcast\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-20T03:00:19"
    },
    {
      "title": "Trump administration asks federal court to dismiss its lawsuit over US Senate election in Alaska – as it happened",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/nov/19/hakeem-jeffries-us-politics-demicrats-republicans-latest-news-updates",
      "description": "<p>This live blog is now closed.</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/nov/19/trump-mary-peltola-alaska-senate\">Trump DoJ moves to drop legal challenge to Mary Peltola’s Alaska Senate win</a></p></li><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/news/2026/feb/17/sign-up-for-the-breaking-news-us-email-to-get-newsletter-alerts-direct-to-your-inbox?utm_medium=ACQUISITIONS_STANDFIRST&amp;utm_campaign=BN22326&amp;utm_content=signup&amp;utm_term=standfirst&amp;utm_source=GUARDIAN_WEB\">Sign up for the Breaking News US email</a></p></li></ul><p>The National Security Agency is seeing<strong> a third day of disruptions to its internal network, NSA Net, which allows staff to share highly classified information with each other</strong>, according to the New York Times.</p><p>The Times reports that employees at the NSA’s Maryland headquarters are under “code orange” for Thursday day shift, which requires non-essential workers are to stay home unless contacted by their supervisors.</p><p><strong>A secret, closed-door election:</strong> Unlike the public speaker of the House vote, where lawmakers cast ballots on the chamber floor, the Senate leadership contest takes place behind closed doors. Senators vote by secret ballot, and individual votes are not disclosed.</p><p><strong>The winner only needs a caucus majority:</strong> The Democratic leadership race is decided solely by members of the party’s Senate caucus. Now that Democrats are set to have 56 seats in the new year, the winner will need secure 29 votes for the top role.</p><p><strong>The vote is scheduled for next month:</strong> As things stand, the election is set for <strong>1 December</strong>. Both incumbent senators and incoming Democratic members will participate, while retiring and outgoing senators will not vote, despite many having publicly weighed in on the race.</p><p><strong>Republicans choose their leaders separately:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans\">Republicans</a> hold their own closed-door leadership elections in both chambers. Earlier this month, House Republicans selected hardliner <strong>Jim Jordan</strong> as their leader after <strong>Mike Johnson </strong>opted not to run for minority leader. In the Senate, Republicans unanimously backed <strong>John Thune</strong> to continue leading the conference.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/nov/19/hakeem-jeffries-us-politics-demicrats-republicans-latest-news-updates\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-20T02:58:52"
    }
  ]
